<Routine Name="R10_Winder_A_Speed_Control" Type="RLL">
<RLLContent>
<Rung Number="0" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[p29r10
This routine is called, continuously, from p29r01 Main while the plc is in Run mode.  All modes and fault reset requests are controlled, here.
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[SBR()OTE(p29r11.comment.0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="1" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Tag Usage

p29r10.comment (dint)
p29r10.oneshot (dint)
p29r10_timer_xxxx (timer)
p29r10_xxxx (any)
p29r10.fault (dint)
p29r10.warning (dint)
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.1);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="2" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Notes:
]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.2);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="3" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[EQU(p29r10.fault,0)OTE(p29r10_run_permissive);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="4" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Requests]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.3);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="5" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29r10_run_permissive)[XIC(p01r13_line_running) ,XIC(p01r13_line_slow_ramp) ]XIC(p29r15_wdra_active)XIC(p29r15_state.6)OTE(p29r10_request_run);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="6" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd) ,XIC(p29r15_wdra_active) XIC(p29r10_index_active) ]OTE(p29r10_index_active);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="7" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[CPT(p29r10_calc_speed_offset,p29_ai_wa_speed-p25_ao_selected_speed);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="8" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[CPT(p29r10_pid_out,p29r10_calc_speed_offset/p29r10_psn_gain+50);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="9" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Auto Start Sequence]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.4);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="10" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Configure PID
The tension loadcell feedback is compared to its setpoint in a PID control.
With the PID output at 50%, error is made to be 0
Error is multiplied to the ramped line speed and added to the ramp line speed to calculate the required speed of the drive.
A gain factor is used to dampen the error effect on the drive speed.(P29R10-10)
The larger the number, the smaller the PID error effect.  Initial setting 60]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.5);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="11" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[TON(p29r10_timer[0],?,?);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="12" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p29_ai_wdr_lc,p29r10_pid_psn_pv);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="13" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p01_recipe_active.winder_tension_sp,p29r10_pid_psn.SP);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="14" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Tension Error and Load Cell PID

p29r10_tension_error is the explicit tension error in lbf.  It replaces
p29r10_pid_psn.ERR everywhere it was consumed (p01r13_winder_tension_ok
and the p29r17 diameter calculation), so no logic outside this routine
depends on PID block internals any more.

The PID itself is OUT OF THE CONTROL PATH.  Winder speed trim now comes
from the proportional accumulator position block below.  The PID is held
in manual permanently with SO tracked at 50 so that it cannot wind up and
the HMI faceplate shows a sane output.  Block and tags are kept declared
for HMI back-compat - delete the PID instruction if the faceplate goes.

Rev 20260820]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[CPT(p29r10_tension_error,p01_recipe_active.winder_tension_sp-p29_ai_wdr_lc);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="15" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Tension Loop Selection - which loop holds web tension

ACCUMULATOR ON   air pressure holds tension.  The carriage position trim
                 drives winder speed, and this PID is parked in manual
                 with its output tracking that trim so the handover is
                 bumpless.

ACCUMULATOR OFF  pressure is zero and the carriage sits on its stop, so
                 nothing pneumatic holds tension.  This PID goes to auto
                 on the winder load cell and trims winder speed - the
                 original pre-accumulator control.

Without this the accumulator work left NOTHING controlling tension when
the accumulator is switched off: speed_offset came only from carriage
position, and with the carriage resting at 0 percent against a 10 percent
park setpoint the trim clamped at its minimum and held the winder at
0.8 x line speed indefinitely.  pid_psn_cv had no consumer at all.

SO is preloaded as offset x gain + 50, the inverse of the offset
calculation, so auto picks up exactly where the trim left off.

Rev 20260904 a38]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p00_wac_enabled)CPT(p29r10_pid_psn.SO,p29r10_speed_offset*p29r10_psn_gain+50);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="16" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(p00_wac_enabled) ,XIO(p29r10_request_run) ]OTE(p29r10_pid_psn.SWM);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="17" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd)MOV(p29r10_accel_bias,p29r10_pid_psn.BIAS);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="18" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd)MOV(0.0,p29r10_pid_psn.BIAS);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="19" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd)MOV(p21r10_index_kp,p29r10_pid_psn.KP)MOV(p21r10_index_ki,p29r10_pid_psn.KI)MOV(p21r10_index_kd,p29r10_pid_psn.KD);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="20" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTU(p29r10_pid_psn.NOBC);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="21" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[If param_psn_gain is too big, fabric will not take up.
If it is too small, you get oscillation.
Big number means small PID error addition to winder speed.
Normal running, need 100.
Turret rotation need 80.
At start of 3" roll, need 40 for about 5 seconds
Use the 3"/6" SS for operator control of faster/slower.
The 3"-6" core switch is used to provide manual pickup speed.]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[NOP();]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="22" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Arm the PID Parameters
Minimum Control Valve Position = x % (per manufacturer)]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p02_real_from_hmi.wdr[40],p29r10_pid_psn.KP)MOV(p02_real_from_hmi.wdr[41],p29r10_pid_psn.KI)MOV(p02_real_from_hmi.wdr[42],p29r10_pid_psn.KD)MOV(p29r10_pid_psn.BIAS,p29r10_pid_psn.BIAS);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="23" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[PID(p29r10_pid_psn,p29r10_pid_psn_pv,0,p29r10_pid_psn_cv,0,0,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="24" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd)MOV(p29r10_param_psn_gain,p29r10_psn_gain);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="25" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29_di_wdr_index_fwd)MOV(p29r10_misc_index_factor,p29r10_psn_gain);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="26" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Divisor guard - psn_gain

psn_gain divides the PID output twice, and param_psn_gain is adjustable
from the HMI.  Zero faults the processor outright; a very small value is
just as bad without the fault - at 0.1 the speed offset reaches +/-500,
which is a runaway rather than a trim.

Floored at 10, which still allows eight times the normal 80 in
aggressiveness and caps the offset at +/-5.

Placed after both selection rungs so it covers the running gain and the
index gain alike.

Rev 20260904 a40]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[LES(p29r10_psn_gain,10.0)MOV(10.0,p29r10_psn_gain);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="27" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Accumulator Position to Winder Speed Trim

Strict proportional, no integral.  Explicit setpoint and explicit gain:

   error = carriage position - p29_param_acc_park_sp
   trim  = error x p29_param_acc_trim_gain
   trim  clamped to [p29_param_speed_trim_min, p29_param_speed_trim_max]

Defaults - park 10%, gain 0.02 per % of stroke, clamp +/-0.20:

        0%  ->  -0.20   winder 0.80 x line, accumulator refills
       10%  ->   0.00   park, winder at line speed, in balance
       20%  ->  +0.20   winder 1.20 x line, drawing down
   above 20%  ->  +0.20  clamped - this IS the payout overspeed

Nothing commands the carriage to the park position.  Park is simply where
the trim crosses zero, and the loop settles there by negative feedback:
above park the winder outruns the line and the carriage falls, and the
trim shrinks as it approaches.  Steady state offset from park is
(persistent surface speed error) / gain - a 1% speed mismatch parks it
0.5% of stroke off, and it clamps only at a 20% mismatch.

This replaces a SYS_SCP block that mapped 0-20% onto -0.20/+0.20.  That
worked, but the park position was implicit - it was the midpoint only
because the two trim limits were symmetric, so changing the negative
authority silently moved where the accumulator lived (trim_min -0.10 with
trim_max +0.20 would have parked it at 6.7%).  Setpoint, gain and
authority are now three independent parameters.

Why proportional only:  carriage position is the INTEGRAL of the speed
difference.  P on an integrating plant is a first order closed loop - it
cannot oscillate on its own and needs no integral term to remove offset.
It also holds no state, so there is no windup and no manual/auto handover.

If it ever hunts at high line speed, widen the band by REDUCING
p29_param_acc_trim_gain - do not reduce the trim clamp, that is the payout
authority and is now a separate number.

The load cell PID does not drive speed.  See the rungs above.

Rev 20260820 a02]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p00_wac_enabled)MOV(p29_param_acc_park_sp,p29_param_acc_park_sp)CPT(p29r10_acc_pstn_error,p00_wac_position_pct-p29_param_acc_park_sp);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="28" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Accumulator Speed Trim - separate gain above and below park

Park sits at 10% of stroke, so the two directions have very different
amounts of travel to work with:

  ABOVE park  90% of stroke.  Gain 0.02 reaches the +0.20 cap at 20%,
              so 100% down to 20% runs flat at 1.20x - the payout
              overspeed - and 20% down to 10% tapers 1.20x to 1.00x.

  BELOW park  10% of stroke.  The same 0.02 could only ever produce
              -0.20 at a bottomed carriage, so trim_min never actually
              clamped anything and the winder could not be slowed by
              more than 20% no matter how empty the accumulator got.

gain_lo 0.05 gives 0.75x at 5% and 0.50x at 0%, with trim_min opened to
-1.00 so the loop can take the winder all the way to a stop.  That is
safe: there is no drive limit slowing down, and the negative-speed block
downstream stops it going into reverse.

Below park the loop is faster - about 5 s versus 13 s above - which is
still well clear of the 25 fpm/s output ramp.  Raise gain_lo on the line
if recovery is too slow, but watch for hunting past about 0.08.

Rev 20260907 a48]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p00_wac_enabled)GRT(p29r10_acc_pstn_error,0)MOV(p29_param_acc_trim_gain,p29_param_acc_trim_gain)CPT(p29r10_speed_offset,p29r10_acc_pstn_error*p29_param_acc_trim_gain);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="29" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p00_wac_enabled)LEQ(p29r10_acc_pstn_error,0)MOV(p29_param_acc_trim_gain_lo,p29_param_acc_trim_gain_lo)CPT(p29r10_speed_offset,p29r10_acc_pstn_error*p29_param_acc_trim_gain_lo);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="30" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(p00_wac_enabled)CPT(p29r10_speed_offset,(p29r10_pid_psn_cv-50)/p29r10_psn_gain);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="31" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Speed Trim Limits

Two different jobs, so two different kinds of limit.

NORMAL RUNNING - fractional.  The trim is drawing the carriage back to
park, and how fast that should happen scales with line speed, so a
fraction is the right shape.  Recovery time then comes out the same at
5 fpm as at 30 fpm.  Limits are param_speed_trim_max / _min.

TURRET INDEX - absolute.  The web path length changes at a rate set by
turret speed and swing radius, in fpm.  It does NOT scale with line
speed.  A fraction is the wrong shape: 0.20 gives 6 fpm of authority at
30 fpm line but only 1 fpm at 5 fpm line, where the disturbance is the
same size.  So during an index the limits become +/- the same absolute
fpm at any line speed:

  trim_hi =  index_trim_fpm / line     ->  winder = line + index_trim_fpm
  trim_lo = -index_trim_fpm / line - 1 ->  winder = -index_trim_fpm

trim_lo goes past -1 on purpose.  Below 1.0 the factor is negative and
the winder reverses, which it must be able to do: at the bottom of the
5-30 fpm range the path lengthens faster than the line feeds.

line_guard floors the divisor at 1.0 fpm.  Line speed reaches 0 whenever
the line is stopped and this rung runs every scan.

Rev 20260825 a26]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p25_ao_selected_speed,p29r10_line_guard);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="32" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[LES(p29r10_line_guard,1.0)MOV(1.0,p29r10_line_guard);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="33" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(p29r10_index_active)[MOV(p29_param_speed_trim_max,p29r10_trim_hi) ,MOV(p29_param_speed_trim_min,p29r10_trim_lo) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="34" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29r10_index_active)[CPT(p29r10_trim_hi,p29_param_index_trim_fpm/p29r10_line_guard) ,CPT(p29r10_trim_lo,0-(p29_param_index_trim_fpm/p29r10_line_guard)-1) ];]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="35" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p00_wac_enabled)sys_Limit_Clamp(p29r10_trim_clamp,p29r10_trim_hi,p29r10_trim_lo,p29r10_speed_offset);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="36" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Output Control]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[OTE(p29r10.comment.10);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="37" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p01_recipe_active.winder_tension_sp,p29r10_pid_tq.SP);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="38" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIO(p29r10_index_active)OTE(p29r10_pid_tq.SWM);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="39" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29r10_pid_tq.SWM)MOV(p29_ai_wa_torque,p29r10_pid_tq.SO);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="40" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.KP,p29r10_pid_tq.KP)MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.KI,p29r10_pid_tq.KI)MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.KD,p29r10_pid_tq.KD)MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.BIAS,p29r10_pid_tq.BIAS)MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.UPD,p29r10_pid_tq.UPD);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="41" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[PID(p29r10_pid_tq,p29r10_pid_psn_pv,0,p29r10_pid_tq_cv,0,0,0);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="42" Type="N">
<Text>
<![CDATA[MOV(p29r10_pid_tq.OUT,p29r10_pid_torque);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="43" Type="N">
