<Rung Number="18" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Stop Dwell - Winder A

The operator presses winder stop.  wdra_active drops, request_run drops
with it, and that is what raises the fill request and closes the nip in
p31.  The winder must NOT stop in that same instant - it has to keep
tension on the web until the nip has actually gripped it.

TOF: DN stays true while request_run is true AND for the preset after it
drops.  So the fill and nip fire immediately, and the drive stays enabled
and at line speed for the dwell, then ramps down normally.

Rev 20260825 a30]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[XIC(p29r15_request_run)TOF(p29r15_stop_dwell,?,?);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
<Rung Number="19" Type="N">
<Comment>
<![CDATA[Winder drive enable - symmetric across A and B

E-stop clear AND this winder's own drive healthy.  Nothing else.

Winder A previously passed p29r15_run_permissive into its VFD AOI, which
carries the whole-line drive permissive AND the turret position window - so
winder A's drive was disabled for the entire doff, and disabled whenever any
other section on the line faulted.  Winder B passed drive_estop_ok, which is
E-stop only and does not check winder B's own faults.

Neither is right.  Turret position belongs on the run REQUEST, where it
already is, not on the drive enable; and the whole-line permissive would
stop you jogging a winder to free the fabric while another section is down.
Own-drive faults do belong here.

Rev 20260825 a12]]>
</Comment>
<Text>
<![CDATA[[XIC(p29r15_stop_dwell.DN) ,XIC(p29r15_request_jog_fwd) ,XIC(p29r15_request_thread_fwd) ,XIC(p29r15_request_thread_rev) ]OTE(p29r15_vfd_start_request);]]>
</Text>
</Rung>
